<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TypeScriptExecutableName Condition="'$(TypeScriptExecutableName)' == ''">tsgo-cs</TypeScriptExecutableName>
    <AssemblyName>$(TypeScriptExecutableName)</AssemblyName>
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../TypeScript.Compiler/TypeScript.Compiler.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
    Performance-tuned GC configuration for the compiler CLI, measured on a 32-logical-processor host.
    The first pass pinned Workstation GC plus a fixed 4 MiB gen0 budget and a 1 MiB LOH threshold.
    Re-measured against the unmodified product baseline, that pinned budget cost the short-lived
    workloads 3-8% (it is a fixed per-process charge paid before Main) while it only paid off on the
    longest runs, so the pinned budget was dropped.
      * Server GC without a pinned budget then measured faster than DATAS on every heavy workload:
        medium -26%, declarations -29%, medium-checkers-8 -31%, huge-file -14%, medium-checkers-1 -14%
        (AB92/AB93, 23 workloads x 15 pairs, CIs excluding 1.0; AB86/AB89 confirmed on 7 workloads).
        DATAS starts with one heap and grows it on demand, and the checker's parallel checkers
        allocate the whole heap within the first moments of the run, which is exactly the ramp-up
        case the runtime documents as allocation waits; it also runs full-compacting GCs to hold
        the heap down. A short-lived process pays that on every run and never banks the memory
        saving, so dynamic adaptation is off.
      * Workstation GC (background and non-concurrent) also beats DATAS on the medium family but
        loses 17-28% on huge-file, where one heap and one GC thread cannot absorb the parallel
        allocation burst of the 32K-object-literal file (AB87/AB88/AB90/AB91).
      * Cost of turning DATAS off is peak RSS on the bursty shapes: huge-file 254 -> 376 MiB and
        the tiny-files family 61 -> 186 MiB (within-batch ratios; absolute values move with machine
        memory state, see the r18 report). The medium family and project references are flat.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>0</GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.Server" Value="true" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
